AB  - Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) represent a significant advancement in artificial intelligence, enabling the integration of audio-visual content, spoken language, and diverse textual data to achieve human-like reasoning and cognitive capabilities. Currently, it is imperative to critically evaluate their architectural designs, training methodologies, assessment paradigms, and real-world applications. Our analysis encompasses more than 100 peer-reviewed studies, addressing both versatile general-purpose frameworks such as GPT-4 V and Gemini, as well as specialized domain-specific models. We delve into the fundamental theories underpinning multimodal learning, recent progress in vision-language fusion architectures, instruction tuning, alignment training mechanisms, and evaluation protocols. Additionally, challenges including the mitigation of hallucinations, computational feasibility, and cross-modal correspondence are systematically examined. This review also highlights underexplored research avenues that could catalyze the advancement of artificial general intelligence within the realm of multimodal systems.
ER  - 

