AB  - In the assessment of fixed asset tax, accurately identifying land use types and building conditions is essential; however, the declining number of municipal staff and growing reliance on individual expert knowledge have made traditional field-based surveys increasingly difficult to conduct efficiently. To address these challenges, this study proposes a land appraisal support system that leverages aerial photographs and parcel boundary data owned by local governments, developed through industry-government-academia collaboration. Specifically, a transformer-based deep learning model is used to classify land use from aerial imagery. Furthermore, when the confidence score of the classifier falls below a predefined threshold, a locally deployed large language model (LLM) automatically generates supplemental explanatory text describing potential decision factors and possible misclassification patterns. Experiments using aerial imagery from Handa City, Aichi Prefecture, demonstrate that the LLM is capable of suggesting plausible alternative land use categories in misclassified cases. These findings indicate that the proposed system can support both the efficiency of field surveys and the accuracy of tax assessments.
