AB  - Clinical text summarization has emerged as an important application of Natural Language Processing (NLP) to efficiently summarize the rapidly growing volume of Electronic Health Records (EHRs) and other medical documents. This survey reviews recent advancements in clinical text summarization by analyzing twenty representative studies that employ transformer-based models, Large Language Models (LLMs), hybrid approaches, semantic graph techniques, multimodal learning, and Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI). The survey compares widely used models, including Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformers (BART), Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer(T5), Pre-training with Extracted Gap-sentences for Abstractive Summarization(PEGASUS), Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers for Biomedical Text Mining (BioBERT), Clinical Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers(ClinicalBERT), Generative Pre-trained Transformer(GPT), and Large Language Model Meta AI (LLaMA), based on their methodologies, performance, strengths, limitations, and evaluation metrics. The analysis indicates that transformer-based and hybrid models produce more coherent, context-aware, and clinically relevant summaries than traditional extractive methods; however, challenges such as factual hallucination, limited explainability, privacy concerns, computational complexity, and the lack of standardized evaluation datasets continue to hinder their practical adoption. Based on these findings, the survey identifies key research gaps and highlights future research directions toward developing accurate, explainable, multimodal, and clinically reliable clinical text summarization systems to support efficient healthcare decision-making and improve patient care.
ER  - 

