AB  - Transformers-based Large Language Models (LLMs) in recent times have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in healthcare, especially in medical image analysis and cancer diagnosis, processing tasks faster. This success of LLMs has managed larger datasets of research contributions in this direction. The integration of diverse cutting-edge LLM technologies like vision transformers (ViTs) and multimodal architectures includes the clinic and laboratory integration program (CLIP) into diagnostic frameworks fetched to report. These models utilize both textual and visual input modalities to detect, segment, and analyze tumors from diverse datasets, including MRI, CT scans, X-rays, radiological reports, and genomic data, leveraging self-attention mechanisms and architectures that integrates LLMs to enhance conventinal workflows. The study explores the approaches of distributed training, hybrid parallelism, and the scalable libraries DeepSpeed and Megatron-LM to report computational challenges in medical data. The applications include cancer detection, anomaly identity, and support clinical decisions and suggest precision medicine. This work demonstrates how AI vision enhances cancer diagnosis accuracy and how transformers work in medical image analysis, clinical data, and genomics. This research presents a roadmap for the integration of transformer-based LLMs with medical image analysis and patient records to generate the AI classifications for future works [7]. This review article is proposed to provide a systematic survey but also a comprehensive reference for the researchers to suggest extensive summary information of existing work to advance in the LLM research.
