AB  - Insider threat detection increasingly relies on artificial intelligence, but traditional deep learning models often depend on large, specialized datasets, making them costly in terms of data collection, computation, and energy. The recent advancements in large language models (LLMs), such as BERT, GPT, and LLaMA, present a promising alternative, enabling sophisticated contextual modeling while reducing the need for task-specific feature engineering. However, a survey of how LLMs are being applied in insider threat detection is still missing. To address the gap, this survey, dedicated to LLM-based insider threat detection, reviews contemporary LLM-based approaches to insider threat detection, categorizing them into BERT-based, GPT-based, LLaMA-based, and hybrid systems. We summarize key techniques covering data serialization, temporal encoding, model adaptation, and evaluation protocols. The review highlights reported improvements over traditional baselines in modeling complex behavioral patterns, mitigating class imbalance, and generating data for rare scenarios. Finally, we identify critical limitations related to external validity, computational efficiency, interpretability, and governance in LLM for insider threat detection, and outline future research directions
ER  - 

