AB  - The rise of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) has significantly advanced the capabilities of AI systems to understand and generate content across diverse modalities such as text, images, audio, video, and sensory data. By leveraging the reasoning prowess of Large Language Models (LLMs), MLLMs unify multiple input formats into a coherent framework, enabling unprecedented performance in multimodal tasks. This survey provides a comprehensive overview of the architectural innovations, training paradigms, data resources, and evaluation benchmarks that have shaped the evolution of MLLMs. We review foundational and emerging models, tracing contributions from both academia and industry, and highlight recent efforts to expand modality coverage, improve cross-lingual support, and enable interactive and embodied AI. Key research themes discussed include multimodal hallucination, multimodal in-context learning (M-ICL), chain-of-thought reasoning in vision-language tasks (M-CoT), LLM-aided visual reasoning (LAVR), and the integration of video, 3D, and tactile data into MLLMs. We also introduce recent benchmarks such as MMBench, SEED-Bench, and MathVista, and analyze trends in aligning models with human preferences via techniques like Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF). Furthermore, we examine persistent challenges—including scalability, multimodal alignment, interpretability, safety, and fairness—and identify open research directions that can guide future exploration in this rapidly evolving field.
ER  - 

