AB  - The relation extraction (RE) task aims to identify the relations between entities in texts. Structured facts extracted through RE can be effectively leveraged for various downstream tasks, including knowledge graph construction and logical reasoning. As this field has evolved over time, it has made significant progress, from deep learning to the rise of large language models (LLMs). However, deep learning-based and LLM-based methods still need to be further explored. Meanwhile, systematic surveys and comparative analysis remain insufficient. Therefore, this paper conducts a comprehensive survey of deep learning-based and LLM-based methods. Based on this, we categorize deep learning-based methods into pipeline-based and joint extraction-based methods, while LLM-based methods are classified into prompt-based and fine-tuning-based methods, providing a detailed introduction to each. In addition, drawing upon the experimental results documented in extant papers, we carry out an in-depth comparative analysis from both experimental and theoretical perspectives. Moreover, to provide valuable references for future research, we introduce several renowned open-source RE tools and explore their features and application scenarios intensively. Finally, we discuss current research challenges thoroughly and outline future research directions. We also provide a public GitHub repository containing curated resources, code links, and supplementary materials related to this survey1.
ER  - 

