AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to intrusion detection in Internet of Things (IoT) environments, yet their practical deployability, robustness, and evaluability remain largely undemonstrated. This article presents a preferred reporting items for systematic reviews and meta-analyses (PRISMA)-based systematic survey of LLM-driven IoT intrusion detection published between 2022 and 2026. A taxonomy is introduced spanning functional roles, model architectures, learning paradigms, deployment models, traffic representations, resource awareness, and robustness evaluation, applied to 122 fully classified studies. Through corpus-wide quantitative analysis, three evaluation gaps in the current research landscape are identified: 1) a deployment gap—38.5% of studies evaluate in explicitly cloud-hosted settings and a further 33.6% omit deployment context entirely; together, 72.1% provide no evidence of IoT edge feasibility and fewer than 14% report inference latency; 2) a comparability crisis—no single benchmark is shared by more than five LLM-based papers, and 73% of studies report accuracy without macro-F1 or per-class recall, rendering cross-study comparison methodologically unreliable; and 3) adversarial blindness—89.3% of approaches are never evaluated under adversarial conditions despite operating in inherently adversarial environments. These findings suggest that current evaluation practices prioritize benchmark accuracy more consistently than deployment-oriented dimensions such as edge feasibility, adversarial robustness, and evaluation comparability. To address this, a minimum evaluation protocol (MEP-IoT-LLM) is proposed, and a research roadmap is outlined, targeting the critically underexplored intersection of edge deployment, adversarial robustness, and continual learning.
ER  - 

