AB  - The rapid growth and deployment of large language models (LLMs) have significantly advanced natural language processing and artificial intelligence, while also raising serious security and privacy concerns. Membership inference attacks (MIAs) ask whether a candidate text unit belongs to pretraining corpora, fine-tuning records, prompts, retrieval databases, long-context inputs, or other LLM data sources. Prior surveys cover general machine-learning MIAs or broader LLM privacy risks, but they do not systematically align LLM-specific membership definitions, cross-setting attack signals, evaluation pitfalls, and the role of memorization. This survey reviews attack-side LLM MIA studies across pretraining, fine-tuning, in-context learning, retrieval-augmented generation, long-context serving, and document-level membership. We complement this usage-based view with a signal taxonomy covering loss-based, likelihood-ratio, reference-calibrated, token-selective, embedding-based, generation-based, and aggregation-based attacks. We further analyze membership-label ambiguity, distribution shift, semantic confounding, and low-false-positive-rate performance. The survey highlights that credible LLM MIA studies require clear membership ground truth, calibrated evidence, memorization-aware interpretation, and metrics suitable for practical privacy auditing.
ER  - 

