AB  - Plagiarism represents one of the most difficult academic problems that have recently become exacerbated, due to the development of digital technologies and specifically the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs), which can generate advanced text. Consequently, the evolution of NLP toward the development of plagiarism detection systems. The current paper aims to make extensive survey problems with various plagiarism detection approaches but the scope of comparative study has been targeted on those research work where PAN Plagiarism Corpus 2011 (PAN-PC11) have considered a standard data-set, thus being able to code significant trends in research during period 2011–2024. The techniques can generally be classified to: traditional statistical methods, intrinsic plagiarism detection methods, machine learning and artificial intelligence methods, early deep learning approaches, advanced hybrid models that leverage both statistics features and deep contextual representations and recent transformer-like architecture based either on Transformer structures or Siamese networks. The present work demonstrates a remarkable improvement in performance as modern hybrid models are capable of achieving much better results than their ancestors. The authors indicate that the best possible combination would be one which leverages the statistical features and the deep semantic learning capabilities of models such as BERT using powerful classifiers like LSTM. Additionally, recent works have also demonstrated that Siamese networks can be taught accurate semantic similarity measurements and points out the necessity of constructing new benchmarks to tackle the plagiarism induced by large language models.
ER  - 

