AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a groundbreaking technology with the potential to reshape numerous aspects of everyday life. The state-of-the-art LLMs like ChatGPT (Generative Pre-Trained Transformer), Gemini, Co-Pilot, Claude, Mistral, BERT and others have made remarkable advancements in various Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks like text generation, translation, summarization, and virtual assistants. A substantial balance must be maintained between utilizing the benefits offered by these models and managing the risks associated with it. These risks comprise of a range of concerns, including bias and privacy, ethical and responsible usage, academic integrity and decline in analytical skills. For deeper understanding of these challenges, a comprehensive study was undertaken comprising of an online survey on 700 Degree College learners of Chandigarh followed by 4 Machine Learning based experiments (Random Forest Classifier, Support Vector Classifier, k Nearest Neighbor and Gaussian Naïve Bayes models were used) on 2 datasets (Virtual Learning Student Interaction Dataset and Student Learning Interaction Logs Dataset). Findings from Survey includes, 66% learners think LLMs helps in personalized answer writing and summarizing. 54.7% learners think excessive use of these LLMs will hamper their critical thinking and problem-solving skills. In experiment, the classes in both datasets were highly imbalanced; therefore, Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) was used to balance the minority class. To validate the accuracy of models, stratified 10-fold Cross Validation was used with training testing ratio as 80:20. The Random Forest Classifier (RFC) algorithm has demonstrated highest accuracy in predicting learner’s engagement and behavioral pattern, around 98.34% and 95.27%, respectively. The study discusses these findings and evaluates their implications in achieving learning outcomes.
ER  - 

