AB  - Serverless computing simplifies application development through abstracting infrastructure management, allowing developers to focus on functionality while cloud providers handle resource provisioning and scaling. However, orchestrating serverless workloads across the edge-cloud continuum presents challenges, from managing heterogeneous resources to ensuring low-latency execution and maintaining fault tolerance and scalability. These challenges intensify when scaling from lightweight functions to compute-intensive tasks such as large language model (LLM) inferences in distributed environments. This tutorial first provides an overview of serverless computing, starting with foundational concepts such as Function-as-a-Service (FaaS) and Backend-as-a-Service (BaaS), and their integration into modern edge-cloud systems. It then explores advanced orchestration techniques for serverless applications operating across the distributed continuum, including dynamic workload distribution, energy- and latency-aware multi-objective scheduling, and scalable deployment of heterogeneous functions, ranging from lightweight microservices to compute-intensive tasks such as LLM inference. Hands-on demonstrations using Kubernetesbased infrastructures, public cloud services (Google Cloud Functions, AWS Lambda, AWS Step Functions), and open-source serverless platforms (OpenFaaS) will provide participants with practical insights. In addition, we showcase the use of monitoring tools (e.g., Prometheus, Grafana) to evaluate serverless orchestration strategies from both performance and energy-efficiency perspectives, enabling participants with theoretical knowledge and practical skills for designing, deploying, and optimizing serverless applications across the edge-cloud continuum.
ER  - 

