AB  - Knowledge distillation (KD) has become a widely adopted approach for model compression of large language models (LLMs) and deep neural architectures by transferring the behaviour of a high-capacity teacher model to a smaller student model. While the classical variant of KD commonly relies on forward Kullback—Leibler (KL) divergence, recent studies have given more emphasis to reverse KL and other divergence measures with respect to increased stability, better mode preservation, and improved alignment with a teacher model's highly confident predictions. In this review we overview major advances in knowledge distillation in different neural architectures including transformers, domain-specific natural language processing (NLP) systems, and reinforcement learning models. We evaluate theoretical justifications for different objectives of divergence, methodological developments, and qualitatively compare the empirical results previously reported on a range of tasks, including medical summarization, legal reasoning, and code generation. Further, this review analyses strengths, limitations, and practical trade-offs of forward KL, reverse KL, and hybrid distillation methods. By distilling knowledge from the literature reviewed, this paper outlines challenges and future research directions regarding the training of efficient, domain-adapted student models suitable for resource-constrained deployments.
ER  - 

