AB  - Natural Language Processing (NLP) has undergone tremendous progress, from initial rule-based systems to the creation of sophisticated large language models (lLMs). Initially, NLP was based on hand-tuned linguistic rules and formalized data sets to handle language. Statistical models and machine learning methods incrementally enhanced the capability to perceive and produce human language more precisely. The advent of deep learning and transformer architectures was a turning point, allowing for more advanced language understanding and generation. This paper discusses the history of NLP technologies, with breakthroughs and their influence on applications like machine translation, sentiment analysis, and question-answering systems. It also discusses the challenges encountered during this shift and the prospects of LLMs in determining future language processing systems. The aim is to present an extensive overview of the evolution of NLP and its revolutionary function in contemporary artificial intelligence.
