AB  - Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, a landmark of recurrent neural networks (RNNs), long dominated the field of sequence modeling. With the rise of the Transformer architecture, however, LSTM was once marginalized due to its difficulties with parallelization and forgetting in long-range dependencies. In recent years, as the context windows of large language models (LLMs) have expanded dramatically, the O(N2) computational and memory complexity of Transformers has become an almost insurmountable bottleneck, prompting the research community to re-examine RNN architectures with O(N) linear complexity. This paper provides a systematic survey of the latest evolution of LSTM and its variants in the "post Transformer era," focusing on the Extended Long Short Term Memory (xLSTM) proposed in 2024, the fusion of LSTM with State Space Models (SSMs), and LSTM–Transformer hybrid architectures. We highlight that by adopting exponential gating and covariance memory rules, modernized LSTMs have attained associative retrieval capabilities and high parallelism on par with Transformers, while demonstrating irreplaceable advantages in edge computing, ultra long time series forecasting, and scientific computing.
