AB  - The evolution of dense image description techniques has progressed from the initial CNN-RNN captioning architectures to more recent architectures based on transformers and diffusion. As compared to previous surveys that mostly present the description of methods, this paper offers the analysis of the way in which next generations of models addressed problems of accuracy, diversity, and interpretability of their predecessors. Methodologies classifications are presented, followed by the discussion of dataset and metric biases and the problem of semantic alignment. The significance of the current trend of using dense descriptions as part of large language models is emphasized. Gemini, Claude Vision, GPT-4o, and LLaVA are examples of multimodal large language models that have incorporated dense descriptions into their general reasoning models, thereby violating the boundary between the area of dense description and conversational multimodal AI.
